Bundled builds link the Rust AV1 decoder in third_party/rav1d into
gkcodecs. rav1d implements the dav1d C API. rust/moz.yaml pins the
upstream revision and applies these patches from rust/:

  rav1d-cargo-packaging.patch   Integrate with Gecko's Cargo workspace.
  rav1d-zerocopy-0.8.patch      Port to the vendored zerocopy version.
  rav1d-stable-build-cpu.patch  Build with stable Rust and fix CPU detection.
  rav1d-gecko.patch             Match the C ABI and preserve DSP alignment.
  rav1d-logger.patch            Match the C va_list logger callback ABI.

Cargo owns the source list, so re-vendoring skips moz.build updates.
rust/Cargo.toml enables all rav1d features.

Bundled builds use the public C headers and fuzz harness from
third_party/dav1d without compiling its C decoder. Keep the headers in
sync with the Rust ABI. rav1d keeps its upstream dav1d_* symbols. Gecko's
Rust wrapper (rust/lib.rs) provides typed moz_dav1d_* forwarders. Gecko
uses the prefixed symbols to avoid collisions with dav1d loaded by system
FFmpeg. --with-system-av1 uses the system libraries.

Update rav1d with:

  ./mach vendor media/libdav1d/rust/moz.yaml -r <revision> --patch-mode check

Refresh the Rust dependencies with ./mach vendor rust, then run
./mach gtest 'Dav1dAbi.*' to check the C ABI and film-grain decoding.
